<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madagascar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madagascar is the world's fourth-largest island, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> off south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Settled by Austronesian seafarers from South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> and by people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> during the first millennium CE, it developed many kingdoms, of which the Merina unified most of the island in the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conquered it in 1895–1896, and it became independent in 1960; its wildlife, such as the lemurs, is found nowhere else.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madagascar.jpg" alt="Madagascar" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Bitton · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madagascar_(centered_orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Madagascar covers 592,800 square kilometres and is the world's fourth-largest island and second-largest island country. It split from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> around 180 million years ago and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> approximately 90 million years ago, so that its plants and animals evolved in relative isolation; over 90 per cent of its wildlife is endemic. A narrow, steep escarpment with tropical lowland forest runs along the east coast, and west of it lies a central plateau of terraced, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing valleys between 750 and 1,500 metres high. The highest point is Maromokotro, at 2,876 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Madagascar was first permanently settled during or before the mid-first millennium CE by Austronesian peoples, presumably arriving by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outrigger-canoe">outrigger canoe</a> from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and scholars are cautious about dates earlier than 250 CE. The first settlers met giant lemurs, elephant birds and Malagasy hippopotamuses, which later became extinct through hunting and habitat destruction. Arab traders reached the island between the 7th and 9th centuries and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Arabic script, adapted as sorabe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> migrants from south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> arrived around the year 1000, and zebu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a> were brought about then.</p><p>Until the late 18th century a fragmented set of shifting alliances ruled the island. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrianjaka">Andrianjaka</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a> as capital of the Imerina Kingdom around 1610 or 1625. From the early 19th century a series of Merina nobles united most of the island as the Kingdom of Madagascar. France's annexation ended the monarchy, and the country became independent in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Madagascar has had four republics and has been a constitutional democracy since 1992. A political crisis and coup in 2009 led to a long transition, and constitutional governance returned in January 2014. Malagasy and French are the official languages.</p><p>The Malagasy form over 90 per cent of the population, divided into 18 ethnic subgroups, of which the Merina of the highlands are the largest at about 26 per cent. The Malagasy language is of Malayo-Polynesian origin, closest to Ma'anyan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. The valiha, the most emblematic instrument, is a bamboo tube zither carried by early settlers from southern Borneo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madagascar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Napoleon III, colonial campaigns added Cochinchina, New Caledonia, and an expanded Senegal under Louis Faidherbe, and the Third Republic later pushed into Tunisia, Tonkin, <b>Madagascar</b>, and the Sahel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the expansionist Jules Ferry the republic acquired Indochina, <b>Madagascar</b>, vast territories in West and Central Africa and much of Polynesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>France's exile of Sultan Mohammed V to <b>Madagascar</b> in 1953 set off active resistance to both protectorates, with the worst violence at Oujda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Merina, the people of the central highlands of <b>Madagascar</b> around Antananarivo, descend from the island's Austronesian settlers, whose ancestors probably crossed the Indian Ocean from the region of Borneo during the first millennium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>British and Commonwealth forces, with Free French troops, took Syria and Lebanon in the five-week campaign that ended on 14 July 1941, and <b>Madagascar</b> from May to November 1942.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Malay belongs to the Austronesian family, which stretches from Southeast Asia across the Pacific and includes Malagasy, spoken far to the west in <b>Madagascar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
